ng the Range of a Parametric Equation"} :step/id #uuid "a2b6d468-dc59-53d1-b89f-9fdbc94f428b" :step/math-academy-id 18604 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088562}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088906 :tutorial/content "We often see parametric curves defined in terms of the parameter $t,$ such as the parametric curve given below:\n\n$$\nx=2 \\cos t, \\,\\,\\, y=3 \\sin t, \\quad 0 \\leq t < 2 \\pi\n$$\n\nHowever, we can also define parametric curves in terms of other variables. Another commonly used variable is $\\theta.$ So, in terms of $\\theta,$ the above parametric equation is expressed as\n\n$$\nx=2 \\cos \\theta, \\,\\,\\, y=3 \\sin \\theta, \\quad 0 \\leq \\theta < 2 \\pi .\n$$\n\nWe often use $\\theta$ when the bounds are $0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi,$ because this implies that the parameter $\\theta$ might be interpreted as an angle." :tutorial/id #uuid "5a132530-6b5f-59d1-bd02-6ac362b558a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3370 :tutorial/title "Defining Parametric Curves Using Other Parameters"} :step/id #uuid "35535a0b-1ada-5085-afad-0de6b32bfb5b" :step/math-academy-id 18844 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088588}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087736 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087542} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cddc940d-2185-569c-a62b-b507e95d3c35" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049872}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698269} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302369} {:db/id #ed